    "description": "Qashqai nomadic life is associated with seasonal pastoral movement in southern Iran and with a confederation of communities of diverse origins. Qashqai identity, pastoral work, and migration are related but should not be treated as interchangeable categories. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Qashqai", "Pastoralism", "Seasonal migration", "Fars", "Zagros", "Cultural heritage"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:27:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:27:44Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Qashqai Nomadic Life: Identity, Movement and Dress | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover the historical context of Qashqai pastoral life in southern Iran, including seasonal movement, cultural identity, and changing dress."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "identity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A confederation, not a single way of life",
        "content": "The Qashqai, also transliterated Qašqāʾi, are a tribal confederation associated with Fars in southern Iran. A confederation brings different constituent groups into a broader social and political association. Encyclopaedia Iranica describes Qashqai clans of Turkic, Lori, Kurdish, and Arab origins and identifies a Western Ghuz Turkic language, locally called Turki, as widely shared. This diversity makes a simple account of a single ancestral population misleading. [s1]\n\nAccounts of early origins also require care. Iranica distinguishes traditions connecting Qashqai ancestors with later invading armies from the author’s interpretation that earlier tribal movements offer a more likely explanation. These are different kinds of evidence: a remembered origin story and a scholarly reconstruction should not be presented as equally established facts. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "movement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding seasonal movement",
        "content": "Historical descriptions place Qashqai pastoral migration between lowland and highland environments in the southern Zagros, the mountain system running through western and southwestern Iran. Seasonal movement was connected with livestock keeping rather than travel without a destination. [s2]\n\nThe broader Iranian pastoral pattern combines grazing areas that become useful at different seasons. Such movement depends on access to land as well as environmental conditions; it cannot be explained by scenery or climate alone. Iranica’s discussion also records adaptations involving settlement, farming, wage work, and motor transport. These observations describe changing Iranian pastoral systems and should not be read as a current census of Qashqai households. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dress-and-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Dress records connections and historical change",
        "content": "Qashqai clothing illustrates how cultural distinction can coexist with exchange. Anthropologist Lois Beck describes garments made using purchased materials and the skills of village and urban specialists. Clothing therefore provides evidence of connections with surrounding markets and craftspeople, rather than isolation from them. [s2]\n\nDress also changed through political intervention. Beck records restrictions on men’s customary clothing under Reza Shah and describes the introduction of the distinctive two-flapped dogushi hat by Naser Khan in 1941. Her account follows variations in women’s clothing into the 1990s. These dated observations help explain why a recognizable garment should not be treated as an unchanged survival from a remote past—or as a requirement that every Qashqai person must meet. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "encountering-living-communities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Meeting people beyond the image",
        "content": "Anthropologist Richard Tapper offers an essential distinction: nomadism concerns movement, pastoralism concerns relationships with livestock and markets, and tribal organization concerns social and political affiliation. These dimensions can overlap without always occurring together. A settled person can retain tribal ties; livestock keeping need not require the whole household to migrate. [s3]\n\nFor visitors, this provides a better starting point than expecting every family to embody a familiar photograph of nomadic life. Ask hosts how they describe their own household, work, and seasonal arrangements. Treat permission to visit or photograph as something to establish directly, rather than something implied by a cultural identity. This is an editorial approach informed by the distinctions above, not a claim about a universal Qashqai hosting custom. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
